ght still be armed because he ran with his right forearm pressed against his hip, \\\"consistent with trying to maintain control of a gun,\\\" Clark said, adding that no gun was found when Harris was searched after the chase. Another video from an officer's body camera picks up the chase. The officer wearing it jumps out of a vehicle and pursues Harris on foot, catching up to him easily. He tackles the fleeing suspect. The officer commands him, \\\"I need you to roll on your stomach. Now!\\\" Other officers appear. Someone calls, \\\"Taser! Taser!\\\" The gun discharges. Much later in the recording made by the first camera inside the truck, two men are conversing. \\\"He thought it was his Taser,\\\" one of them says, as the other curses in frustration. In the background, a woman is crying \\\"No, no, no!\\\" Harris had come to the sting deal with a female companion. Police have said that Harris had reached for his waistband, and officers feared he might endanger them. When Harris was on the ground, he \\\"refused to pull his left arm from underneath his body where his hand was near his waistband,\\\" they said after the shooting. The officer's body camera video did not reveal that area of Harris' body. Bates, a former Tulsa police officer, was placed on administrative leave after the shooting, the sheriff's office has said. Asked whether Bates' age may have been a factor in the shooting, Clark said, \\\"It's happened to 21-year-old law enforcement officers. It's happened to 30-year-old law enforcement officers. Age is not really a factor in consideration for the dynamics behind slip and capture events. Despite the details released over the weekend, Harris' brother says he still want answers. \\\"I want to know if he was shot in the back accidentally or on purpose. These are all the things that not only I want, but the public wants,\\\" Andre Harris told CNN affiliate KTUL. Bates' attorney, Scott Wood, told CNN on Friday he would contact the network later. He had not issued a statement or called back as of Sunday. CNN's Jason Morris, Carma Hassan and Andreas Preuss contributed to this report.\\n\", \"answer\": \"Tulsa County Reserve Deputy Robert Bates, who shot a suspect with his firearm instead of his stun gun, and another deputy who cursed at the suspect after he was shot, were not in their normal states of mind due to the elevated stress of apprehending the suspect, according to Tulsa Police Sgt. Jim Clark. Clark, who has been brought in to review the case, said that Bates \\\"inadvertently\\\" shot Eric Courtney Harris, a possibly PCP-addled felon who had tried to sell an illegal handgun during an undercover sting. Bates arrived at the scene with a pepper spray gun and announced that he was going to use a Taser on the suspect, but instead, he fired a single gunshot and immediately apologized. Clark attributed Bates' actions to a phenomenon known as \\\"slip and capture,\\\" where someone intends to do one thing but accidentally does another. Bates expressed surprise and remorse after realizing he had shot Harris. Clark defended the use of explicit language by the officers, saying that they experienced auditory exclusion and thought the suspect was out of breath from running. Clark emphasized that Bates had done nothing criminally wrong and was a victim of slip and capture. The shooting was captured on video, which was released by the Tulsa County Sheriff's Office. Harris later died at a local hospital. The investigation is ongoing, and Harris' brother is seeking answers about the incident. Bates has been placed on administrative leave.\"}<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\n\n</think>\n\n","generated":""}
