rnal solid-state drives: Solid-state drives use a feature called TRIM.When you delete a file from a solid-state drive, the operating system informs the drive that the file was deleted.indows 7 doesn\u2019t have this wiping feature built-in. If you just reinstall Windows 7 on your PC using a Windows 7 installer disc or your PC\u2019s recovery feature, your drive won\u2019t be wiped. Deleted files could theoretically be recovered from your drive. DBAN's scary warning isn't lying: Nuke is in the name for a reason.\\\\n\\\\n\\\",\\\"question\\\":\\\"recovering files from a wiped hard drive\\\"}\", \"answer\": \"Based on the provided passages, it is possible to recover files from a wiped hard drive, depending on how the drive was wiped. If the drive was only deleted or quickly formatted, then recovery programs like Recuva may be able to recover the files. However, if the drive was wiped with a program that overwrites everything, then the files are likely gone forever. Additionally, if the drive has been reused and Windows has been reinstalled, then the files may be overwritten and unrecoverable.\\n\\nIt is important to note that internal solid-state drives (SSDs) use a feature called TRIM, which helps to recover marked-as-deleted data until it is overwritten in the future. Therefore, it may be more difficult to recover files from an SSD than from a traditional hard drive.\\n\\nUnable to answer based on given passages.\"}"}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "seed": 70553, "max_tokens": 128, "cache_prompt": false, "chat_template_kwargs": {"enable_thinking": false}}
