d in the other, you have Jesus \\\"Chuy\\\" Garcia, a county commissioner who has come to embody populist and liberal Democrats' frustrations with the Chicago incumbent. After Emanuel failed to snare half the vote in February's general election, he will go head-to-head with Garcia. The timing is interesting, too, as Easter, Passover and spring break appear to have spurred more than 142,000 early votes, up from about 90,000 before the first round of voting in February. Following Michael Brown's death, the national spotlight shone on Ferguson, particularly how the city's predominantly black population is woefully underrepresented in its police force and City Council. Yet with all the hubbub about the face of civic leadership, only four in 10 city residents hit the polls in November to cast ballots. Residents speak out ahead of vote. Tuesday's election will bring change, no matter how the ballots are cast: Two black men are running for one of the open seats, and the current lone black council member isn't up for re-election. In another ward, two black women and two white men are vying for an open seat. And a white protester is running for a third post.\\n\", \"answer\": \"The US was the stage for a number of newsworthy events on Tuesday. In Massachusetts, two high profile court cases are drawing to a close. The trial of Dzhokhar Tsarnaev, the accused Boston Marathon bomber, has reached the deliberation process. If found guilty on any of the 17 capital counts against him, Tsarnaev may face life imprisonment or the death penalty. Meanwhile, the trial of former New England Patriot, Aaron Hernandez, for the murder of semi-pro player, Odin Lloyd, has also reached closing arguments and the jury will soon begin deliberations. In Kentucky, Senator Rand Paul announced his candidacy for the presidential elections. In Chicago, voters are participating in a runoff between Mayor Rahm Emanuel and challenger Jesus \\\"Chuy\\\" Garcia. In Ferguson, Missouri, the shadow of Michael Brown and the protests over his shooting by Officer Darren Wilson loom large over the city's elections.\"}<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n<think>\n\n</think>\n\n", "add_special": true}
